in this lesson we're going to talk about  the sine and cosine graphs these are  oftentimes referred to as sinusoidal  graphs and they're basically like an s  shape type graph but let's talk about  the basic format of the equation so when  you look at the sine or cosine graph it  basically falls into this format and you  can see the number that's in front of  her to the left of the sine or cosine  that a value that's called the amplitude  that's how high the waves are  from the midline okay and then the b  value here that's used to calculate the  period the periods how long it takes for  the graph to repeat okay or to complete  one cycle and the way you can figure  that out from the equation or from the  formula is by taking 2 pi which is the  normal period divided by this b value  this number that's in front of her to  the left of the x okay and then the h  the number is grouped with the x that  shifts the graph left and right in the  horizontal direction but it's opposite  from the sign so if this was like x  minus pi it would actually shift it  right pi if it was x plus pi it would  actually shift it left pi to the to the  negative to the left pi  and the k value is what's shifting it up  and down that's the vertical shift if  it's plus two it'd go up two minus two  down two that one has the same effect on  the graph as the sine but the one group  of the x has the opposite effect so  that's an overview of you know the sine  and cosine graphs let's get into some  example problems see if you can solve  these on your own and we'll go through  them so the first one says what is the  amplitude of the sine graph y equals 3  sine 2x plus 4  well  you can see that this number in front of  the sign that's your amplitude so the  answer would just be three if they ask  you for the period the period would be  two pi divided by two which is just pi  if they ask you for the horizontal or  vertical shift this one's just shifting  up four so it'd be a vertical shift up  four so that's just kind of knowing the  you know the layout the format of the  equation there the second example they  give us a graph and it says what is the  period of the sinusoidal graph shown  okay so see if you can do this one you  can see they're giving us a few choices  a b c d  okay so the way i would do this is i  would look at you know how long does it  take for it to complete one cycle so you  can see it's going up  down  and back up so from zero  to four pi that's how long it took for  it to complete one cycle or repetition  so the period here is going to be four  pi letter d  you don't have to measure from here to  here you can measure from the peak to  the peak that's a good way to do it or  from the low point to the low point  that's a good way to do it you can see 7  pi minus 3 pi is giving you a period of  4 pi so more than one way to do that  in this case you can see the amplitude  is actually three so you're measuring  from this midline okay halfway so that  that's the height of the waves it's like  if you were standing on the the beach  here that's how high the waves are not  from here to here it's the max minus the  min divided by two  and the amplitude is always positive  so that's a quick review of working with  sine and cosine graphs i'll see in the  next lesson
 

